  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern across the CONUS early this morning shows an \namplified trough axis associated with mid-level low pressure off the \ncoast of northern California, and broad southwest flow with moisture\ntransport across the Four Corners and toward the southern/central \nHigh Plains. Shortwave energy is moving to the east of the area. \nStratus looks to hold over the area today but southerly flow and low\nlevel WAA ahead of the west coast system will allow for some warming\nto near 70 degrees. WAA and divergence aloft could be enough lift \nfor a few light rain showers today, but will keep chances low.\n\nThe western system will quickly progress eastward, inducing lee \ncyclogenesis this evening and tonight. POPs increase tonight and \nMonday morning from west to east with an initial area of PVA ahead \nof the main trough axis. A 50 to 60 kt LLJ sets up tonight and sfc \nwinds begin to increase as the pressure gradient increases. Gusts \npick up as boundary layer mixing commences Monday morning, with some \n40 to 45 mph gusts possible throughout the day. Instability will \nincrease slightly with moisture advection, but overall instability \nstill appears fairly limited at less than 1000 J/kg along and ahead \nof an approaching front. Shear parameters still look impressive. \nHREF has effective shear around 50 kts and low level plenty of SRH \nto support rotating updrafts. With ample shear and forcing, the \nseverity of storms that develop in central KS later in the day will \nlargely depend on the amount of instability present. Still, \nconfidence is good in at least some initial supercells that will be \ncapable of damaging wind and a few tornadoes. Storm mode may favor \nmore clusters or linear segments eastward with a damaging wind threat\ncontinuing. The severe threat should diminish toward the MO state \nline as instability wanes during the late evening hours.\n\nModels agree more on the mid-level low becoming cutoff over the area \non Tuesday, possibly keeping some wet weather around, especially as \nthe low pivots to the north and east. Eventually, high pressure \nmoves in as the low approaches the Great Lakes, bringing a more \nseasonable cool and dry air mass to northeast Kansas. Lows are \nexpected to be in the 40s with highs in the 60s through mid and late \nweek. POPs then increase again next weekend.",
